{"page_content": "Reference QSAR models Abraham et al. (2003) Draize rabbit eye test compatibility with eye irritation thresholds in humans: a quantitative structure-activity relationship analysis. Barratt (1995) The role of structure-activity relationships and expert systems in alternative strategies for the determination of skin sensitisation, skin corrosivity and eye irritation. Gerner et a/. (2005) Assessment of the Eye Irritating Properties of Chemicals by Applying Alternatives to the Draize Rabbit Eye Test: The Use of QSARs and In Vitro Tests for the Classification of Eye Irritation. Solimeo et al. (2012) Predicting Chemical Ocular Toxicity Using a Combinatorial QSAR Approach. Content Draize rabbit eye test scores, as modified maximum average score (MMAS), for 68 pure bulk liquids were adjusted by the liquid-saturated vapor pressure P. These 68 adjusted scores, as log (MMAS/P), were shown to be completely equivalent to eye irritation thresholds (EIT), expressed as log (1/EIT), for 23 compounds in humans. Thus, for the first time the Draize eye test in rabbits for pure bulk liquids is shown to be perfectly compatible with eye irritation thresholds in humans. This paper describes the derivation of a set of structural alerts for skin sensitisation, which have been incorporated into the expert system DEREK, and of Quantitative structure-activity relationships (QSARs) derived for predicting the skin corrosivity (for organic acids and bases) and for the eye irritation potential (for neutral organic chemicals). This paper evaluates and discusses the nature of eye lesions and their importance for classification and labelling of possible hazards to human eyes, with a view to promoting the development of specific in vitro assays which are able to discriminate between eye damage, moderate eye irritation, and minor irritation effects which are completely reversible within a few days. Structural alerts for the prediction of eye irritation/corrosion hazards to be classified and labelled according to international classification criteria, are presented, which should be validated in accordance with internationally agreed (OECD) principles for (Q)SAR system validation. Physicochemical limit values for prediction of the absence of any eye irritation potential relevant for human health can make available a definition of the applicability domains of alternative methods developed for the replacement of the Draize eye irritation test. This paper describes QSAR models for a set of small molecules with animal ocular toxicity data compiled by the National Toxicology Program Interagency Center for the Evaluation of Alternative Toxicological Methods.", "metadata": {"doc_id": "161b29be-118d-4b98-b1ce-b6cc6c9a8f22", "content_type": "Table", "filename": "information_requirements_r7a_en_part_2.pdf"}}